\section{Error Analysis \emph{[Exploratory]}}
\label{sec:analysis}

The following failure mode taxonomy emerges empirically from LLM-coded traces, distinct from the input ambiguity taxonomy (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

We analyze 614 violation traces across all five models, coded into five failure modes (Gwet's AC$_1$ $= 0.62$, $\kappa = 0.52$ \citep{gwet_2014,cohen_1960}). Using LLMs to code LLM failures introduces circularity risk; however, mutual information between type (exogenous) and mechanism labels ($I(\text{type}; \text{mechanism}) = 0.41$ bits, $V = 0.45$; Appendix~\ref{app:type_mechanism}) provides structural evidence against this. Two of the six type--mechanism mappings reflect partial definitional affinity (authorization scope$\to$boundary violation, incompleteness$\to$gap-filling); the remaining four (e.g., scopal$\to$structural misparse, 82.7\% cross-model consistency) are empirically derived.

\subsection{Type-Specific Failure Mechanisms}

The five-model data reveal three mechanistically distinct failure patterns that crosscut the specification/linguistic layer boundary.

\emph{Structural misparse}: the agent commits to an incorrect parse, paralleling garden-path processing \citep{frazier_rayner_1982,ferreira_good_enough_2002}. Dominates scopal (5/5), conditional precedence (4/5), and coreferential (3/5).

\emph{Information gap-filling}: the agent fills missing information with plausible but incorrect defaults. Dominates incompleteness (3/5) and lexical (3/5).

\emph{Boundary violation}: the agent exceeds permission boundaries when authorization scope is underspecified; 69.2\% involve unauthorized escalation with zero conservative refusals ($p < 10^{-4}$, binomial). Dominates authorization scope exclusively (5/5).

All dominant modes achieve $\geq$3/5 model consistency; scopal and authorization scope are unanimous (5/5).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_type_mechanism.pdf}
\caption{Type--mechanism association (percentage of violation traces per type assigned to each failure mechanism). Each type maps to a dominant mechanism ($\geq$3/5 model consistency), supporting the taxonomy's diagnostic value (NMI $= 0.23$, Cram\'{e}r's $V = 0.45$).}
\label{fig:type_mechanism}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Disambiguation Neglect}
\label{sec:dn}

Agents rarely recognize ambiguity. We code externally expressed \emph{disambiguation awareness} versus \emph{surface adoption} (proceeding with a single interpretation). Overall awareness is 4.6\%. Awareness clusters by model: GPT-4.1 and Claude~Sonnet~4.6 show non-zero rates (10--11\%) while Qwen3-235B and DeepSeek-V3 exhibit zero awareness across all 278 combined violation episodes ($p = 2.2 \times 10^{-9}$, Fisher's exact test); GPT-5.4 falls in between at 4.1\% (Appendix~\ref{app:awareness_breakdown}).

We term this pattern \emph{disambiguation neglect}---the agentic-context manifestation of what \citet{liu_ambiguity_2023} call ambiguity collapse \citep{hu_ambiguity_2025}, here producing irreversible policy violations rather than mere response inaccuracy. The absence reflects default behavior, not inability: interactive disambiguation recovers resolution when enabled \citep{vijayvargiya_ambigswe_2026}, and a prompt-based mitigation experiment (Appendix~\ref{app:mitigation}) confirms type-dependent effectiveness: authorization scope ($-$18.0\,pp), lexical ($-$8.9\,pp), but not incompleteness ($-$3.3\,pp, n.s.), reinforcing policy-design over prompt-level fixes.

\subsection{Systematic Nocuity}

We adopt `nocuous' from \citet{berry_kamsties_2004}, noting an inversion: where they describe \emph{divergent} human interpretations, our agents exhibit \emph{convergent} errors. Among ambiguous clauses, 41.3\% trigger majority-convergent violations ($\geq$3/5 models; 13.0\% unanimous)---a phenomenon we term \emph{systematic nocuity}: models converge on the same incorrect interpretation, invisible in agreement-based evaluation. Of these, 83.9\% share the dominant failure mode, confirming convergent---not coincident---violations. Incompleteness has the highest universal rate (24\%, OR $= 2.61$, $p = 0.019$; subject to the information-addition confound; \S\ref{sec:results}).

\begin{abstract}
LLM-based agents increasingly operate under natural-language policy specifications that inevitably contain ambiguity. We present a matched-pair causal study on $\tau^2$-bench with 300 ambiguous/unambiguous clause pairs spanning a six-type taxonomy, evaluated across five models. Ambiguous clauses are consistently nocuous (i.e., harmful), increasing violation rates by 34.0 percentage points ($+$18.7 to $+$36.0\,pp in confound-free types). Clause wording explains 22.4\% of violation variance; however, type predicts failure \emph{mechanism} more strongly than violation rate, establishing the taxonomy as a diagnostic tool. Error analysis reveals disambiguation neglect and \emph{systematic nocuity}: models converge on identical policy violations. These results, observed in customer-service domains under controlled synthetic ambiguity, motivate clause-level policy auditing and type-targeted diagnosis for policy-governed deployments.
\end{abstract}

